# Store text files with LF line endings, whatever the editor or OS uses. This also holds in a Windows checkout, even
# with Git's core.autocrlf=true (the eol attribute wins): the bash scripts and the Containerfiles must keep LF, since
# bash and the Linux build containers would take a CR as part of a command. Windows PowerShell 5.1 runs the .ps1
# scripts with LF endings too (keep them ASCII: without a byte order mark it reads them in the ANSI code page).
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
Containerfile text eol=lf
